    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Spiritists believe in one God, in the immortality of the spirit, in reincarnation as the means of moral and material improvement, and in useful communication with spirits through mediums.</li><li>The doctrine rests on five works by Allan Kardec, the Spiritist Codification of 1857–1868, and has no priestly hierarchy: its basic unit is the Spiritist centre, a non-profit association.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> has the most Spiritists, about 3.8 million in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2010, and there the doctrine was banned by the penal code of 1890 until the code of 1940 ended the prohibition.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Kardec’s own account of table-turning is that, to obtain longer answers, he had the table knock a number of times for each letter of the alphabet; asked what it was, the being that answered declared itself a spirit.</p></aside>
    </section>

