    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-faraday.jpg" alt="Michael Faraday" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael_Faraday_sitting_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kardecist spiritism, also called Kardecism or simply Spiritism (Espiritismo in Portuguese and Spanish, spiritisme in French), is a reincarnationist and spiritualist doctrine established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the mid-19th century by the writer and educator Hippolyte Léon Denizard Rivail, who wrote as Allan Kardec. He coined the term spiritisme in 1857 and defined it as “the doctrine founded on the existence, manifestations, and teachings of spirits”. It emerged as a new religious movement in tandem with spiritualism, and ideas and practices of communication with spirits had spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> since the 1850s.</p><p>Spiritists believe that mediumistic phenomena are universal and have always existed, and they cite biblical cases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a>’s consultation of the Witch of Endor. In the 19th century attention to mediumship grew in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Europe, in the form of strange noises, knocks on furniture and objects that moved without apparent cause, as in table-turning. Many Spiritists mark 31 March 1848, the beginning of the events at the home of the Fox sisters in Hydesville, as the first milestone of modern mediumistic manifestations.</p><p>Kardec observed table-turning and, to obtain longer answers, had the table knock a number of times for each letter of the alphabet. The being that answered declared, when questioned about its nature, that it was a spirit. He questioned whether muscular action could explain such movements, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a>’s experiments, published in 1853, showed that table-turning was caused by the ideomotor effect. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>, in exile on Jersey from 1851 to 1855, took part in many sessions and came to believe he had contacted his late daughter Léopoldine and writers such as Shakespeare and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Spiritism was founded, in the doctrine’s own dating, on 18 April 1857, with the publication of The Spirits’ Book, which presents dialogues with disembodied spirits who speak through mediums from the standpoint of Christian morality. Its basic principles include the existence and unity of God, whom Spiritists regard as the supreme intelligence and primary cause of all things, and the immortality of the spirit, which acts on matter through a “semimaterial” link called the perispirit. Spirits return to matter as many times as needed in order to improve; Spiritists reject metempsychosis. All spirits are held to be created equal, “simple and ignorant”, with free will and a destiny of perfection, and a law of cause and effect governs their fate. Spiritism also teaches a plurality of inhabited worlds.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, held to be created by God, is the guide and model of humanity, and Spiritists do not ascribe a divine nature to him. They reject the Trinity and salvation by grace, and they summarise Christian morality as “Outside of charity, there is no salvation”, charity meaning benevolence towards all, indulgence towards the imperfections of others and forgiveness of offences.</p><p>The doctrine has no priestly hierarchy and no institutionalised rituals such as baptism or a marriage ceremony. It excludes exorcism, sacramental formulas, horoscopes, amulets, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, vestments, altars and offerings to images, and all its practice is free of charge. It has no official symbol; the vine branch engraved in The Spirits’ Book is taken as an image of the relation between spirit and body.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spiritist Codification consists of five basic works published between 1857 and 1868. The Spirits’ Book (1857) contains the doctrine’s fundamental principles. The Mediums’ Book (1861) treats the investigative side of Spiritism and the phenomena its followers attribute to spirits. The Gospel According to Spiritism (1864) reads the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical gospels</a> from the Spiritist standpoint, with attention to worship, prayer and charity. Heaven and Hell (1865) examines the fear of death, heaven, the Christian hell, purgatory and eternal punishment, and includes dialogues that purportedly took place between Kardec and various spirits. The Genesis (1868) takes up the creation of the universe, the nature of the spirit and of so-called miracles.</p><p>Kardec’s complementary works include What is Spiritism? (1859), an introductory work, and the periodical Revue Spirite, which he founded in January 1858 and directed until he died in 1869; the International Spiritist Council now publishes it quarterly. In January 1890 the directors of the Parisian Society of Spiritist Studies released his unpublished writings as Posthumous Works. Sic Cogito (1892) by B. P. Hașdeu is a work on Spiritism as a philosophy.</p><p>Kardec described Spiritism as at once a science of observation and a philosophical doctrine, and also, in other writings, as religious. Spiritists do not agree on whether it is a religion. At the International Spiritist Congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1925 a proposal to remove its religious aspect was opposed by Léon Denis, who called it not the “religion of the future” but the “future of religions”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1857 to 1869, the year of Kardec’s death, Spiritism gained 7 million followers, by one count. It reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in the decades of 1850 to 1860, where it attracted the intellectual elite with its proposals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a>, anticlerical reform and free thought; Refugio Indalecio González published El Evangelio Según el Espiritismo in Spanish there in 1872. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the Marquis of Flórida read a bill for its official teaching on 26 August 1873, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> B. P. Hașdeu, after the death of his daughter Iulia, practised it at Câmpina and wrote Sic Cogito in 1892.</p><p>It arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in the 1860s; the Brazilian Spiritist Federation dates it to 1865. Through Bezerra de Menezes and Chico Xavier it became popular across much of the country. Brazil has the most followers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, where Spiritism has been present since the 19th century, had 400 Spiritist centres in 2011 with 200 more being registered.</p><p>Spiritism is also associated with other currents: it influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a>, Umbanda and New Age movements. The term “Kardecist spiritism” arose partly to distinguish Kardec’s doctrine from Afro-Brazilian religions such as Umbanda, which had begun to call themselves Spiritist. The Pan-American Spiritist Confederation, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> on 5 October 1946, brings together Spiritists mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The basic unit of organisation is the Spiritist centre, also called a Spiritist society or house. In law it is an ordinary non-profit association, run by a president or directors elected for a term, and its members fund the centre and its charitable activities. Centres differ from Spiritualist churches in not being organised as ecclesiastical bodies, and many offer “spiritist complementary therapies”. Spiritists are strongly associated with charity, maintaining asylums, orphanages, schools and daycares; the Brazilian Spiritist Federation, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> on 2 January 1884, gathers about ten thousand institutions.</p><p>The International Spiritist Council, constituted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 28 November 1992, unites national movements and has 35 associated countries; it holds the World Spiritist Congress every three years. The Pan-American Spiritist Confederation defends a secular view of Spiritism and takes positions other Spiritists contest, such as separating the doctrine from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> the penal code of 1890 banned the practice of Spiritism and punished it with up to six months in prison; it ceased to be officially prohibited only with the code of 1940. Dissenting currents include Roustainguism, which accepts Jean-Baptiste Roustaing’s The Four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> had a fluidic body; Christian rationalism, which began in Santos in 1910; Ramatism; Conscientiology, systematised in 1987; and Christian Renewal, since 2002.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>About 13 million Spiritists are reported worldwide for 2005, and the International Spiritist Council counts 36 member countries. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded about 3.8 million, up from 2.3 million in 2000, making Spiritists the third-largest religious group there, with the highest income and education levels of the census’s groups. Kardec is the most read French author in Brazil, with more than 25 million copies of his books sold, and Spiritist books lead bestseller lists. Brazilian television and film have drawn on Spiritist works, among them the soap opera A Viagem and the film Chico Xavier.</p><p>Spiritists also maintain a movement of health care, including the International Spiritist Medical Association, Spiritist hospitals and the physician Adolfo Bezerra de Menezes’s book on mental disorders, Insanity from a New Perspective.</p><p>Its standing is disputed. Spiritists regard their doctrine as Christian; the sociologist Antônio Flávio Pierucci says it is not a Christian religion, and many scholars call it neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The Catechism of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> says Spiritism often implies divination or magical practices. The scientific consensus considers parapsychology a pseudoscience, while Alexander Moreira de Almeida calls Kardec’s approach scientific. The Theosophical Society saw Spiritist explanations as too simple, and René Guénon criticised its concept of reincarnation in The Spiritist Fallacy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Spiritism is a reincarnationist doctrine founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1857 by Allan Kardec, who held that spirits communicate through mediums and return to life to improve. Its five basic works are the Spiritist Codification of 1857 to 1868, and it has no priesthood. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> has the most followers, about 3.8 million in 2010.</em></p>

    <section class="ty-sec" id="test-yourself" aria-labelledby="ty-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="ty-title">Test yourself</h2>
